What happens: in one lathe setup, two threading operations (default parameters), both generated and Ok. Save the project, load it into a fresh world: the first op (TH0) reads Ok, the second (TH1) reads Stale with reason "operation 'TH0' ahead of it in the program changed - regenerate the program in order" (`rest.rs`, `prior_stale`). Reproduced on origin/main 64506ff5.

Expected: a project that was all Ok when saved loads all Ok (the #169 law: save and load do not stale an op).

Cause (traced, not fixed): TH1's stamp digests TH0's cached path through `Toolpath::path_sig` (`crates/app/src/path_sig.rs`, bit-exact hash of every move field), stored when the cache is built. The generated TH0 path carries `y = 6.050000000000001`; the project file writes moves through the lattice snap (`MoveDto`, `cl::lattice`) and the loaded cache has `y = 6.05`. Same 28 moves, first differing move is index 0, so the loaded `path_sig` differs (6467650602549402493 vs 17772775613656745229 for TH1's own, TH0 likewise) and TH1's stored rest digest no longer matches. Any op whose generated coordinates are off the lattice, followed by a dependent op in the same chain, would go Stale the same way; threading is where it shows (the first op of a chain is unaffected as it digests no predecessor).

Reproduction (scratch test, a copy of `crates/app/tests/turning_thread.rs` with its helpers `thread_world`, `create`, `thread_params`, `status`, plus):

```rust
#[test]
fn probe_two_threading_ops_reload() {
    let (world, mut history) = thread_world();
    create(&world, &mut history, "TH0", "TD-1732-60", thread_params());
    create(&world, &mut history, "TH1", "TD-1732-60", thread_params());
    assert_eq!(status(&world, "TH0"), OpStatus::Ok);
    assert_eq!(status(&world, "TH1"), OpStatus::Ok);
    let b = std::env::temp_dir().join(format!("probe-stale-{}.json", std::process::id()));
    camcraft_app::project::save(&world, &b).expect("saved");
    let other = new_world();
    camcraft_app::project::load(&other, &b).expect("loaded");
    let _ = std::fs::remove_file(&b);
    for n in ["TH0", "TH1"] {
        assert_eq!(status(&other, n), OpStatus::Ok, "{n}"); // TH1: left Stale, right Ok
    }
}
```

Likely fixes to weigh: compute `path_sig` over the snapped moves at generation (so the stored cache equals what a load gives), or snap the generated path when the cache is built. Either must keep save-load-save byte identity.

Found while refreshing #517 / #468 / #518 (the thread infeed stack): its test `the_plan_rides_the_project_file_and_a_default_one_is_absent` fails on this, and on main the same defect exists without that stack.

Done means: the probe above passes (both ops Ok after load), pinned in the turning_thread suite, and the project-file roundtrip suites stay green.

Found by M3's delta review of PR #1287 (comment 2026-11-12T23:03Z). It was posted after #1287 merged at 23:00Z, so this is on main now.

**What happens.** `install_panic_hook` in `crates/ui/src/win_console.rs` (about lines 135-144) fires at panic time, before any `catch_unwind` runs. The app catches panics on purpose in several places:
- `crates/ui/src/egui_app/gpu_init.rs:166` (the GPU probe);
- `shell.rs:910` and `shell_window.rs:220`;
- `crates/app/src/geometry/import/step.rs:133` (a STEP kernel panic turns into a clean import error);
- `crates/app/src/mcp/mod.rs:155`;
- `crates/kernel/src/truck_impl.rs`.

So when the app is started by double-click on Windows, a *handled* panic still pops a modal "CamCraft stopped unexpectedly" box. A bad STEP import is one example. The box blocks that thread and uses up the once-per-process flag, so a later real failure shows nothing.

A second, smaller case: if `AttachConsole` succeeds but the `CONOUT$` open fails (around lines 95-97), `CONSOLE_ATTACHED` stays true. Output then goes nowhere, and no box is shown.

**Done means:**
- the global hook is gone, and on Windows `main` wraps `camcraft_ui::run` in `catch_unwind`, showing the box only for a panic that escapes `run` or a non-zero exit with no console;
- a failed `CONOUT$` open counts as "no console";
- any pure part (payload to text) has a unit test;
- the PR's Windows hand-check list includes "a bad STEP import by double-click shows no box".

The MCP server (`camcraft-cli mcp`) lets an AI agent drive CamCraft, but the only user-facing mention is two sentences in `guide/posting.md` and `developers/commands.md`. The real guide is the `camcraft-mcp` skill in `.claude/skills/`, which users won't find. Add a guide page for it.

Cover:
- what it is and what an agent can do with it (build a job, add tools and operations, generate, verify, post, undo), and that every MCP tool runs the same undoable commands as the GUI;
- the two ways to run it:
  - headless on stdio (`camcraft-cli mcp`);
  - attached to the open app (`camcraft-ui --mcp-port <port>` plus `camcraft-cli mcp --connect 127.0.0.1:<port>`), so the user watches the agent work live;
- setting it up in an MCP client (a generic `command`/`args` config example, without depending on one vendor's client);
- the tool list by area (job, tools, operations, setups and WCS, picking, posting), generated from the server's own `tools/list`, or with a note on where to get the current list;
- an example session: load a project, add a tool, add a pocket, generate, verify, post, with the calls and short answers;
- safety: the agent's output is held to the same refusals as the GUI; posted G-code is still proved on the machine the usual way.

Done means: an "AI agents (MCP)" page in the guide that a user can follow to connect an agent, with both run modes, a client config example, the tool list and an example session.

Add one page listing every keyboard shortcut and mouse action in CamCraft, with a column each for Windows/Linux and macOS.

Cover:
- File, Edit and Run shortcuts (new, open model, open project, save, save as, post, undo, redo, copy and paste an operation, delete, frame/fit);
- the viewport mouse (orbit, pan, zoom, and the trackpad equivalents on a Mac);
- picking modifiers (add an edge, pick a whole loop, box-select, deselect);
- right-click and context menus (Control-click or two-finger click on a Mac).

Take the bindings from the code (`crates/ui/src/egui_app/panes.rs`, `edge_pick.rs`, `interact.rs`, the ribbon tips), not from memory. Where the app uses egui's `command` modifier, the macOS column is Cmd. Where it reads the real Ctrl, check on a Mac whether Control-click arrives as a right-click, and say so.

Related: #1236 (Mac equivalents throughout the site) finds the Windows-only wording on other pages; this page is the one table those pages can link to.

Done means: a shortcuts and mouse page in the guide, every entry checked against the code, with Mac and Windows/Linux columns, linked from first-run.md.

Add a troubleshooting page to the guide, organised by **what the user sees** rather than by rule (`guide/refusals.md` explains the rules; this page answers "I got this message, now what?").

For each entry:
- the message as the app shows it (or its stable start, for messages that name an operation or a value);
- what it means in plain words;
- the usual causes;
- the fix, step by step, with the setting to change and where it is.

Start with the refusals and problems users meet most. Pull them from the refusal strings in the code (posting, verify, gouge and collision, holder and workholding, reach and travel, fidelity) and from recent user-reported issues. Also cover a few non-refusal problems: the app won't start or shows a blank window (GPU, #74), a model imports at the wrong scale, an operation stays Stale, or a post writes nothing.

Keep the message text in sync: a later PR that changes a listed message should update the page. A test that checks each listed message stem still exists in the source would catch drift cheaply.

Done means: a troubleshooting page with at least 20 entries covering posting, verify, setups, tools and import, each with message, meaning, cause and fix, linked from refusals.md and the guide index.

Part of #290. Found while working #532 (Wire EDM: 2-axis contour op).

**What happens:** the Wire Contour op (#532) cuts ONE CLOSED profile, offset to a stated punch / die side. An open profile - a cut-off line, a slot or a relief entered from the stock's edge - is refused by name ("the profile is open ... an open profile has no punch or die side"), because punch and die are not defined for a run that encloses nothing.

**Wanted:** an open run cut by the wire, with:
- the kerf side stated as left / right of travel (or "on the line" for a slitting cut, where the kerf is centred) - explicit, never inferred, the same law as punch / die;
- the wire entering from a stated threading point outside the stock, or from a start hole, and leaving at the run's end (or returning);
- the same corner minimum on the side the wire rides inside a corner, and the same kerf law (no move brings the wire nearer the kept side than wire radius + gap);
- both ends of the run stated: where it starts and whether it runs out past the stock.

**Not in this issue:** tabs (#539), taper (#536), skims (#538).

**Open design points** (to settle on this issue before code): how the kept side of an open run is named in the op's parameters next to `side: die | punch`; whether a run that ends inside the material needs a relief hole stated at its end.

**Done means:** an open run cut with the kerf on a stated side stands exactly the kerf offset off the line on that side (pinned by a test and a CL golden); a run with no stated side, and one whose inside corner is tighter than wire radius + gap, are refused by name; the closed-profile goldens of #532 do not move.

Found while working #869 (Air coolant mode). An operation's coolant (`Off` / `Flood` / `Mist` / `Air` / `Through tool`) is set in the GUI through the undoable `EditOperationParams::with_coolant` (`CommandKind::EditOperationCoolant`), but no MCP tool or script verb reaches it. A headless agent cannot ask for air on a Carvera, or for Off on a dry cut.

**Done means:**
- an MCP tool (e.g. `set_operation_coolant { name, coolant: "off" | "flood" | "mist" | "air" | "through-tool" }`) that runs the same undoable command, so undo/redo hold headlessly;
- an unknown word is refused by name;
- the camcraft-mcp skill's tool table lists it;
- pinned by a test that sets Air through MCP and undoes it.

Intended label: `enhancement` (gh won't let this account add labels).

**Today:** the only feature-based WCS placement is the Geometry page's "Align WCS (3-2-1)" button (#187, `crates/app/src/pick/align.rs`): it reads the open operation's pick chips with fixed roles - one flat face becomes Z (outward normal), one straight edge becomes X (projected into that plane), one vertex or bore centre becomes the origin. That means:

- no role choice - a face is always Z and an edge is always X. You cannot put a bore's axis along WCS X, or stand Z on an edge;
- a cylinder's wall can only contribute its centre as the origin - its axis never aligns to anything;
- the picks land in the open operation's geometry chips, so aligning the WCS pollutes the operation's feature list.

**Expected:** a WCS-from-features pick mode where each pick carries a chosen role - Origin, X, Y or Z - over flat faces, straight edges, vertices and curved walls (cylinder patches):

- a flat face gives a plane (outward normal), a straight edge a direction, a vertex a point, a curved wall a fitted axis and centre (the axis fit from wall facets is new: `circle_of_region` fits circles only on level rings of vertical walls, there is no general axis fit today);
- any two distinct axis picks fix the orientation, the third axis is derived right-handed by cross product; the Origin pick is optional, and without it the origin falls on a sensible feature point;
- degenerate picks refuse by name: two axis picks too near parallel to separate, a doubled role, gone features say re-pick, and the #366 winding refusals carry over.

**Done means:** an armed "Set WCS from features" pick mode following the Setups page's "Lay on a plane" pattern (#330: `crates/ui/src/plane_pick.rs` + `setup_plane_pick.rs`): arm, viewport clicks land in the draft (never in operation chips), and a live-gated Apply queues the existing `CommandKind::SetWcs` - one undo step, no new command kind. Tests pin the role table, every named refusal, and a cylinder-axis fit on a bore turned off-axis. The existing "Align WCS (3-2-1)" button stays as it is.

Provenance: user-assigned datum alignment as in common fixture practice; all math stays our own.

Part of #290 (design decisions D1-D10 in its research comment).

Depends on: #531.

The first wire op (D1). A vertical cut along a contour:
- offset by the wire radius plus the condition's gap;
- explicit **punch / die** side parameter, never inferred (folded in per D8);
- lead-in/out from a stated **start hole / threading point**;
- **corner strategy:** minimum internal radius is wire radius + gap, and a smaller corner is refused by name (folded in per D8).

Moves carry no pose, so the cut posts as XY (D2). Refusals: an enclosed profile without a start hole; a missing wire diameter, gap or condition (at command creation).

**Done means:** a die and a punch on the same profile offset to opposite sides by exactly the kerf. A too-tight corner and a missing start hole are refused by name. CL golden committed.
